**Non-blocking findings**

- **Status-format compatibility passes.** [bin/router:104](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/router:104) watches file timestamps; [bin/status:11](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/status:11) prints status output. No additional field-sensitive job-status parser was found in `eval/`. The new `read` accepts old three-field lines with an empty PGID. [bin/memguard:159](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/memguard:159) parses wrapper argv, whose layout remains compatible.
- **Stable-snapshot self-protection and the RSS floor are correct.** Ancestors and the watchdog’s own wrapper tree are excluded at [bin/memguard:133](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/memguard:133) and [bin/memguard:153](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/memguard:153). [bin/memguard:269](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/memguard:269) suppresses MemAvailable-only kills below 256 MiB; aggregate-RSS violations bypass that floor.
- **OOM-priority implementation is structurally sound, apart from identity races.** Targets are adjusted before trigger evaluation. The wrapper’s builtin write precedes workload creation, so children inherit `1000`. `setsid` followed by `nohup` preserves the wrapper argv, and `systemd-run --scope` inherits the caller’s execution environment. Detached child sessions remain the completeness issue described above.
- **Mail delivery failure is silent and can stall polling.** [bin/memguard:289](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/bin/memguard:289) has no timeout and ignores the sender’s exit status. Orphaned marked jobs also lose their job-log association when `job_name()` returns `None`.
